    </ul>

    <article class="handbook-section" id="who">
      <h2>Who can do what</h2>
      <p>
        Everyone in the group sits in one of four tiers. They stack: each one
        can do everything the tier above it can, plus a little more.
      </p>

      <h3>Member</h3>
      <p>
        Everyone who registers with the group passcode. Members read and post on
        the discussion board, upload drafts, read other people's drafts, and
        comment page by page. This is the whole group — most people never need
        anything else.
      </p>

      <h3>Session host</h3>
      <p>
        A member who is running one particular meeting — the person who says
        where the group is sitting that day. It is a per-meeting job, not a
        standing rank: it hangs off a single date, not off someone's account,
        and it ends by itself when that date passes. See
        <a href="#hosts">Session hosts</a> for what they can and can't change.
      </p>

      <h3>Leader</h3>
      <p>
        The volunteers who run the site: everything in this admin console.
        Announcements, the weekly schedule and one-off meetings, the map pin,
        booting and restoring <em>members</em>, reset codes for members, the
        reader watermark, the group passcode, backups. A leader can also make
        another member a leader.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Leadership can be temporary.</strong> On
        <a href="/admin/members">Members</a>, "Make leader" comes with a
        length: <em>Permanent</em>, <em>1 week</em>, <em>1 month</em>, or
        <em>Until date…</em> for anything else. A temporary leader shows in the
        table as <span class="mono">LEADER · until Aug 17</span>. When that
        moment passes the site quietly puts them back to member — no button to
        remember, no awkward conversation. They are <em>not</em> signed out and
        they lose nothing they wrote; the admin console simply stops being
        there on their next click. Promote them again any time. Use it for
        someone covering while you're away, or a new co-leader on a trial run.
      </p>

      <h3>Architect</h3>
      <p>
        One person, the owner of the board — whoever first set the site up.
        The architect has every leader power, plus the ones that point
        <em>downward</em>: only the architect can remove someone's leadership,
        boot a leader, or generate a reset code for a leader. Ordinary leaders
        see no buttons at all on another leader's row, and the site refuses
        those requests even if someone tries to send one by hand.
      </p>
      <p>
        The architect's own account is protected in both directions: nobody —
        not another leader, not even the architect themselves — can deactivate
        it, demote it, or force a reset code onto it. That is the safety net.
        Because there is always exactly one architect, and the architect can
        never be removed, this site can never lock itself out, no matter who
        gets demoted or booted.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Handing it over.</strong> Stepping back from the group, or
        passing the site to whoever is taking over? At the bottom of
        <a href="/admin/members#transfer">Members</a> there is a
        <strong>Transfer the architect role</strong> box — visible only to the
        architect. Pick the person, type <span class="mono">TRANSFER</span> in
        capitals, and confirm. They become the architect; you become a
        permanent leader. Everyone stays signed in and nothing else on the site
        changes. It is one-way: after that only <em>they</em> can hand it back.
        If you only want another pair of hands in the admin console, promote
        them to leader instead — that is the everyday tool; this one is for
        succession.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="handbook-section" id="joining">
      <h2>How members join</h2>
      <p>
        Give someone the site's web address and the group passcode (see
        <a href="/admin/passcode">Group passcode</a>) — that's the whole invite.
        They visit <span class="mono">/register</span>, pick a username and
        password, type the passcode, and they're in as a member.
      </p>
      <p>
        The passcode is shared by the whole group, not per-person, so anyone who
        has it can join. If it leaks to someone outside the group, change it on
        the <a href="/admin/passcode">passcode page</a> and share the new one at
        the next meeting — existing members keep their accounts; only new
        sign-ups need the new code.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="handbook-section" id="weekly">
      <h2>The weekly schedule</h2>
      <p>
        The group's standing meetings — Saturdays in front of Hilo, Mondays at
        the r. bar — are set up once as <strong>weekly meetings</strong> and then
        run themselves. Nobody has to type in next week's date, ever. They live
        at the top of <a href="/admin/meetings#weekly">Meetings</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Weekly meeting vs. one-off meeting.</strong> A weekly meeting is
        a rule: "every Monday at 5:30 PM, at the r. bar." A one-off meeting is a
        single date: a workshop, a holiday party, a Thursday read-through. You
        can have both — the front page always shows whichever happens
        <em>soonest</em>, so a one-off on Wednesday will out-rank the Saturday
        session, and after it passes the weekly rhythm takes over again on its own.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Adding one.</strong> Click "Add a weekly meeting," pick the day
        and the start time (Baltimore time — daylight saving is handled for you),
        give it a title, say where you sit in plain words, and click the floor map
        to drop the red pin. Save, and the front page is already showing the next
        one.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Skipping one week.</strong> Group away for the holiday? On the
        Meetings page, each weekly meeting has a "Skip a date" box already filled
        in with the next date it would happen. Click "Skip that week" and that
        one occurrence disappears — the front page jumps straight to the next
        meeting after it, and the rule keeps running as normal. Skipped dates are
        listed underneath with an <strong>Undo</strong> button if plans change
        back.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Pausing or removing.</strong> "Deactivate" pauses a whole rule
        (a summer break, a venue closing) — it vanishes from the front page and
        comes back the moment you click "Activate." "Delete" removes the rule for
        good, along with its skipped dates; use it only when a meeting really
        isn't coming back.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Moving the pin.</strong> The stall the group sits in front of is
        part of the rule, so click "Edit" on the weekly meeting and click a new
        spot on the map — every future occurrence uses the new pin. If a single
        week moves somewhere else, it's usually easier to skip that date and add
        a one-off meeting with the new spot.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="handbook-section" id="meetings">
      <h2>One-off meetings and the map pin</h2>
      <p>
        Go to <a href="/admin/meetings">Meetings</a> and click "Schedule a
        meeting." Pick the date and time, add a short note if you like ("bring
        printed pages"), and click on the floor map where the group is sitting
        — that drops the red marker. The public front page always shows the
        <em>next</em> upcoming, non-cancelled meeting — weekly or one-off,
        whichever is sooner — so you don't need to clean up old ones; just add
        the new one.
      </p>
      <p>
        Didn't get a table yet? Save the meeting without clicking the map — the
        front page will show the date and time with no marker until you edit it
        later and click a spot.
      </p>
      <p>
        Plans changed? Open the meeting from the Meetings list and either move
        the marker and save, or mark it cancelled — cancelled meetings drop off
        the front page but stay in the list so you can see what happened.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="handbook-section" id="offsite">
      <h2>Off-site events</h2>
      <p>
        Sometimes the group meets somewhere that isn't R. House — a themed
        night at somebody's house, a reading at another venue. On the
        <a href="/admin/meetings/new">meeting form</a>, the first choice is
        <strong>R. House</strong> or <strong>Off-site event</strong>. Pick
        off-site and the floor map is replaced by three things: an
        <strong>address</strong>, a <strong>details</strong> box (plain text or
        simple markdown, same as an announcement), and up to five
        <strong>attachments</strong> — a flyer, a photo of the block, a parking
        map. JPG, PNG, WebP or PDF, 10 MB each.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>The privacy rule — this is the important part.</strong> An
        off-site event's <em>address, details and attachments are shown to
        signed-in members only</em>. Nobody else. A visitor who is not signed in
        sees the title, the date and the time, and a line saying "Off-site
        event — sign in for the details." Nothing more reaches them: the
        address isn't hidden with styling, it is never sent to their browser at
        all, and the attachment links don't exist for them. Somebody's home
        address never sits on a public page.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two things to keep in mind. The <strong>title</strong> is public, so
        call it "Worldbuilding night," not "Party at 14 Elm St." And the
        <strong>Notes</strong> field is the ordinary public one that every
        meeting has — the private text goes in <strong>Details</strong>, which
        is marked <span class="mono">members only</span> right on the form.
      </p>
      <p>
        Editing an event again later lets you tick <strong>Remove</strong> next
        to any attachment and add new ones. Cancelling an event pulls it off the
        front page and its files stop opening for anyone, members included.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="handbook-section" id="hosts">
      <h2>Session hosts</h2>
      <p>
        A host is a member you hand <em>one</em> session to. They're the person
        on the floor that day, so they're the person who should be able to say
        "we're on the patio" without texting a leader. Nothing about their
        account changes and nothing carries over to the next week.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Handing one over.</strong> For the weekly schedule, each rule on
        <a href="/admin/meetings#weekly">Meetings</a> has a "Give one session to
        a host" box: pick a date from the next few, pick the member, click
        <strong>Assign host</strong>. It works exactly like skipping a week —
        per date, never "every Saturday forever." For a one-off meeting, the
        <strong>Host</strong> dropdown is on the meeting form itself.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>What a host can do.</strong> They get a page at
        <span class="mono">/host</span> listing what they're running in the next
        30 days. For each one they can set the <em>start time</em>, <em>where in
        the room</em> the group is sitting, the <em>map pin</em>, and a
        <em>note for the day</em> ("we're on the patio today"). That's the whole
        list, and it applies to that date only — the weekly rule is untouched,
        so the following week goes back to normal on its own.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>What a host cannot do.</strong> Everything else: the title, the
        kind of event, an off-site address, cancelling, other dates, other
        people's sessions, and anything at all in this admin console. The site
        refuses those requests rather than hiding the buttons, so there is
        nothing to be careless with. A session in the past goes read-only.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Taking it back.</strong> Set the host to <strong>Nobody</strong>
        (or click Remove next to the assignment). Removing a host from a weekly
        date also <em>undoes the changes they made to that date</em> — the pin,
        the label, the time and the note all go back to what the weekly rule
        says, because those changes only existed for the person running it.
        Handing the same date to a <em>different</em> member instead keeps the
        arrangements, so the new host starts from what's already been set up. On
        a one-off meeting there is nothing to undo: a host edits the meeting's
        own fields, which were always yours to edit too.
      </p>
      <p>
        The host's first name shows on the front page — "Hosted by Dana" —
        publicly, so people know who to look for. Only the name.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="handbook-section" id="announcements">
      <h2>Announcements</h2>
      <p>
        <a href="/admin/announcements">Announcements</a> is a simple bulletin
        board on the front page — visible to everyone, even people who haven't
        joined yet. Give it a title and write the body in plain text or simple
        markdown (blank line between paragraphs, <span class="mono">**bold**</span>,
        a dash for a list). "Pin" keeps an announcement at the top above
        everything else; only pin the one or two things that matter most right
        now. Removing an announcement is a soft delete — it disappears from the
        site but isn't destroyed.
      </p>
      <p>
        The front page's "The Collective" intro paragraph works the same way —
        edit it any time at <a href="/admin/about">About the group</a>.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="handbook-section" id="chat">
      <h2>Chat, groups and private messages</h2>
      <p>
        <a href="/chat">Chat</a> is the quick, talky end of the site — the board
        is for conversations worth keeping, chat is for everything else. There
        are three kinds of conversation and they behave differently:
      </p>
      <h3>#general</h3>
      <p>
        One room, everybody in it, automatically. Nobody joins or leaves it and
        it can't be archived. New members see the whole history the day they
        register.
      </p>
      <h3>Project groups</h3>
      <p>
        Any member can start one ("Sci-fi crew," "Graphic novelists") with a
        name and a one-line description. Anyone can see that a group exists and
        join it; only members who have joined can read what's in it. The person
        who started the group — or any leader — can rename it or archive it.
        Archiving is the gentle end: the group drops off the joinable list and
        stops taking new messages, but every message stays readable to the
        people who were in it, and it can be reopened.
      </p>
      <h3>Private messages</h3>
      <p>
        Two members, one conversation, and <strong>nobody else can read it —
        including you</strong>. Leaders have no view into private messages, the
        architect has no view into private messages, and there is no admin
        screen that lists them. Asking for the URL of someone else's private
        conversation returns "page not found," not "not allowed," so the site
        won't even confirm that a conversation exists. Inside a private
        conversation only the person who wrote a message can remove it; a leader
        can't reach in and delete somebody else's line.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is deliberate, and it means your moderation tool for private
        messages is the same one you'd use for anything else somebody does off
        the site: if a member reports harassment, you
        <a href="#members">deactivate the account</a>. Deactivation signs them
        out everywhere immediately and blocks them from signing back in. Take
        the report seriously, act on the person, and don't promise anyone you
        can go read the messages — you can't, and neither can the person who
        built this.
      </p>
      <h3>Attachments</h3>
      <p>
        Any message can carry up to three files, 10 MB each — images (JPG, PNG,
        WebP, GIF), PDF, Word, plain text, markdown, or a zip. It's meant for
        sharing a handout or a prompt sheet, not for hosting a library; drafts
        for critique still belong in the <a href="/drafts">draft library</a>,
        where the reader and the page comments live. Files are checked by their
        actual contents, not their name, and every download runs through the
        same sign-in check as the rest of the site — a chat file link pasted
        anywhere outside the site is dead.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="handbook-section" id="members">
      <h2>Booting and reactivating members</h2>
      <p>
        On <a href="/admin/members">Members</a> you'll see everyone who has
        ever registered. "Deactivate" boots someone: it signs them out of every
        device immediately and blocks them from signing back in, but keeps
        their posts and drafts intact. "Reactivate" undoes it — they can sign
        in again with their old password.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can also promote a member to leader from the same table, for good
        or for a set length of time — see
        <a href="#who">Who can do what</a> for how the tiers fit together, and
        for who is allowed to boot or demote whom. Booting a <em>member</em> is
        any leader's call; anything aimed at a <em>leader</em> belongs to the
        architect alone, and the architect's own row can't be acted on by
        anybody, which is what keeps the group from locking itself out.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="handbook-section" id="reset-codes">
      <h2>Password reset codes</h2>
      <p>
        There's no email on this site, so password resets go through you. What
        you are handing out is <strong>temporary access</strong>, not a
        password: leaders never see, choose, or set anyone's real password, and
        nobody — not a leader, not the architect — can read an existing one.
        Only the member themselves ever types the password they end up with.
      </p>
      <p>
        When someone forgets theirs, find them on
        <a href="/admin/members">Members</a> and click "Generate reset code."
        A one-time code appears on screen for 48 hours — copy it and text it to
        them right then, because it is <strong>only shown once</strong>. They
        go to <span class="mono">/reset</span>, enter their username and the
        code, and pick a new password of their own. The code dies the moment it
        is used.
      </p>
      <p>
        Codes expire automatically after 48 hours and a fresh code replaces any
        unused one, so it's safe to generate a new code if the first text
        didn't go through. Reset codes for <em>leaders</em> can only be
        generated by the architect, and no code can ever be forced onto the
        architect's account.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="handbook-section" id="watermark">
      <h2>The reader watermark</h2>
      <p>
        The draft reader can tag every page with a faint, unselectable overlay
        of whoever is currently reading it — their display name and username,
        tiled lightly across the page. It's off by no penalty to reading and on
        by default. Toggle it from the <a href="/admin">admin dashboard</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The point isn't to stop someone from screenshotting a page — it's to
        make a leaked screenshot traceable back to whoever's account it came
        from. See <a href="#protection">below</a> for the honest limits.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="handbook-section" id="backups">
      <h2>Backups</h2>
      <p>
        Click <strong>Download backup</strong> on the
        <a href="/admin">admin dashboard</a> once a month or so — right after a
        meeting is an easy habit to build. It downloads a single zip file
        (named <span class="mono">afwc-backup-YYYY-MM-DD.zip</span>) straight
        to your computer; nothing is stored anywhere else.
      </p>
      <p>Inside the zip:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <span class="mono">app.db</span> — the whole database: every member,
          post, announcement, meeting, and comment.
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="mono">uploads/drafts/</span> — every uploaded draft file
          (the originals, not just the converted pages), organized by draft.
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="mono">uploads/events/</span> — anything attached to an
          off-site event: flyers, maps, menus.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Together those two things are the <em>entire</em> site — if the server
        ever needs to move to a new host, or something goes badly wrong, this
        zip plus a fresh copy of the app's code is everything needed to bring
        it back exactly as it was. Keep a couple of recent backups somewhere
        safe (a laptop, cloud storage, wherever you'd keep anything else you
        don't want to lose) rather than just the newest one.
      </p>
      <p>
        The dashboard shows how long it's been since the last backup and turns
        an amber warning color once it's been over 30 days (or if one has
        never been taken) — a nudge, not an alarm.
      </p>
      <p>
        Restoring is a hosting-level, slightly technical step (unzip the
        backup into a fresh data volume) — see the "Backup and restore" section
        of the project's <span class="mono">README.md</span> for the exact
        commands. It's rare enough that it isn't worth automating into a
        button, but it's a handful of copy-paste steps, not a rebuild.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="handbook-section" id="protection">
      <h2>What draft protection can and can't do</h2>
      <p>
        Members' drafts are locked behind sign-in — nobody outside the group
        can open a draft link, originals are never downloadable, and the PDF
        and image readers have no download or print buttons. That stops casual
        sharing and keeps the site itself from handing out copies.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is honest to say what it <strong>doesn't</strong> stop: anyone
        determined enough can photograph or screenshot their screen, and
        there's no technical way to prevent that — no web app can. The
        watermark exists for exactly this gap: it doesn't block a screenshot,
        it makes one traceable to the account that took it, which is usually
        deterrent enough inside a small trusted group.
      </p>
      <p>
        In short: the real protection here is <em>membership</em> — only
        people the group has vouched for (via the shared passcode) can read
        anything at all — plus attribution as a backstop. It was never meant
        to be bulletproof against a member who wants to leak something on
        purpose.
      </p>
    </article>

    <p class="section-foot">
      <a class="btn btn--sm" href="/admin">Back to admin</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
